.\" Manual page for bogentag. @VERSION@ is filled in by build-deb.sh.
.\"
.\" Author: David M. Anderson
.\" Built with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic)
.TH BOGENTAG 1 "2026\-11\-09" "bogentag @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bogentag \- the time read from the Sun's arc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bogentag
.RB [ \-\-demo
.RB [ \-\-at
.IR RFC3339 ]
.RB [ \-\-place
.IR LAT , LON ]
.RB [ \-\-dial
.IR spans | kiloseconds ]
.RB [ \-\-precision
.IR low | medium | high ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Bogentag
shows the time in Bogenzeit. Each half of the Sun's arc holds 100 spans:
sunrise to solar noon, solar noon to sunset, and the same on either side of
solar midnight. The Spans dial draws every day the same shape and reads the
position in spans; the Kiloseconds dial moves the Sun evenly and reads the
time from the nearest anchor in kiloseconds.
.PP
A click on the dial shows UTC, the local clock with its zone, the date, and
the day's sunrise and sunset. The Day page gives the lengths of the day and
night arcs and the times of the day's four events. The timer counts down in
spans or kiloseconds and the desktop announces its end; the stopwatch counts
up, with laps. About Bogenzeit explains the standard.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-demo
Show Houston at 17:08 UTC on 1 November 2026, the worked example of the
Bogenzeit specification, and save nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-at " RFC3339"
With
.BR \-\-demo ,
show this instant instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-place " LAT,LON"
With
.BR \-\-demo ,
show this place instead, in degrees, north and east positive.
.TP
.BI \-\-dial " spans|kiloseconds"
With
.BR \-\-demo ,
open on this dial.
.TP
.BI \-\-precision " low|medium|high"
With
.BR \-\-demo ,
read the dial at this precision.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/bogentag/settings.json
The dial, the precision, the place and the last timer amounts.
.TP
.I ~/.config/bogentag/timing.json
The running or paused timer and the stopwatch, kept across restarts.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns success when the window has been closed. A non\-zero status means the
window could not be created at all: no display, or no usable graphics driver.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bz (1)
.SH AUTHOR
David M. Anderson <david.anderson@excelano.com>
